{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-104208" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038052/assets/2499715807dedd0234363454dea33afb592d497005c4f643b03145b6c89cc222.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the surface shown above in cylindrical polar coordinates?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038052/state.json" "file_sha256" "642ab815f48a1942583b110a577e3c48cdd1fc29aa868eabc132fd5320a77e43"} {"question" "q-104208" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The position of a point in cylindrical polar coordinates is written as $(r,\\theta ,z),$ where \n\n- $r\\ge 0$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ are the usual plane polar coordinates, and\n\n- $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ is the usual Cartesian $z$-coordinate.\n\nThe picture shows a half-plane that passes through the $z$-axis.\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- If we project the vector $\\overset{\\to }{OP}$ onto the $xy$-plane for every point $P$ on our surface, the resulting vector makes an angle of $2$ radians with the positive $x$-axis. Therefore, we must have $\\theta =2$ for every point on the surface.\n\n- On the other hand, $r$ and $z$ can vary arbitrarily in their respective domains.\n\nTherefore, the equation of the surface in cylindrical polar coordinates is $\\theta =2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038052/state.json" "file_sha256" "642ab815f48a1942583b110a577e3c48cdd1fc29aa868eabc132fd5320a77e43"} {"question" "q-104208" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038052/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d96e9c0c18cfc1671ca2507634917d13a07ac0f22b2225cb7e810ba8b2f8885c"} {"question" "q-104208" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038052/state.json" "file_sha256" "642ab815f48a1942583b110a577e3c48cdd1fc29aa868eabc132fd5320a77e43"} {"question" "q-104208" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\theta =2"] ["math" "\\theta =2r"] ["math" "r=2"] ["math" "z=2"] ["math" "z=2\\theta "]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038052/state.json" "file_sha256" "642ab815f48a1942583b110a577e3c48cdd1fc29aa868eabc132fd5320a77e43"} {"question" "q-104208" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\theta =2"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038052/state.json" "file_sha256" "642ab815f48a1942583b110a577e3c48cdd1fc29aa868eabc132fd5320a77e43"} {"question" "q-104204" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038052/assets/bfd160c9286927c9cafdf19fb591eba6b1c1a49ee0362c15c5a2fd26d50cdbc6.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the surface shown above in cylindrical polar coordinates?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038052/state.json" "file_sha256" "642ab815f48a1942583b110a577e3c48cdd1fc29aa868eabc132fd5320a77e43"} {"question" "q-104204" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The position of a point in cylindrical polar coordinates is written as $(r,\\theta ,z),$ where \n\n- $r\\ge 0$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ are the usual plane polar coordinates, and\n\n- $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ is the usual Cartesian $z$-coordinate.\n\nThe picture shows a circular cylinder of radius $1$ whose axis of symmetry is the $z$-axis. \n\nWe note the following:\n\n- Since the distance from any point on our surface to the $z$-axis equals $1,$ we must have $r=1$ for every point on the surface.\n\n- On the other hand, $\\theta $ and $z$ can vary arbitrarily in their respective domains.\n\nTherefore, the equation of the surface in cylindrical polar coordinates is $r=1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038052/state.json" "file_sha256" "642ab815f48a1942583b110a577e3c48cdd1fc29aa868eabc132fd5320a77e43"} {"question" "q-104204" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038052/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d96e9c0c18cfc1671ca2507634917d13a07ac0f22b2225cb7e810ba8b2f8885c"} {"question" "q-104204" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038052/state.json" "file_sha256" "642ab815f48a1942583b110a577e3c48cdd1fc29aa868eabc132fd5320a77e43"} {"question" "q-104204" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\theta =1"] ["math" "r=1"] ["math" "r=\\theta "] ["math" "z=1"] ["math" "z=r"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038052/state.json" "file_sha256" "642ab815f48a1942583b110a577e3c48cdd1fc29aa868eabc132fd5320a77e43"} {"question" "q-104204" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "r=1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038052/state.json" "file_sha256" "642ab815f48a1942583b110a577e3c48cdd1fc29aa868eabc132fd5320a77e43"} {"question" "q-104209" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038052/assets/e2d50dc387d24ef2656adf0f6de402b5778416f6c82ce3d593fd0be8a777584a.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the surface shown above in cylindrical polar coordinates?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038052/state.json" "file_sha256" "642ab815f48a1942583b110a577e3c48cdd1fc29aa868eabc132fd5320a77e43"} {"question" "q-104209" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The position of a point in cylindrical polar coordinates is written as $(r,\\theta ,z),$ where \n\n- $r\\ge 0$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ are the usual plane polar coordinates, and\n\n- $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ is the usual Cartesian $z$-coordinate.\n\nThe picture shows a half-plane that passes through the $z$-axis.\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- If we project the vector $\\overset{\\to }{OP}$ onto the $xy$-plane for every point $P$ on our surface, the resulting vector makes an angle of $\\frac{11\\pi }{8}$ radians with the positive $x$-axis. Therefore, we must have $\\theta =\\frac{11\\pi }{8}$ for every point on the surface.\n\n- On the other hand, $r$ and $z$ can vary arbitrarily in their respective domains.\n\nTherefore, the equation of the surface in cylindrical polar coordinates is $\\theta =\\frac{11\\pi }{8}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038052/state.json" "file_sha256" "642ab815f48a1942583b110a577e3c48cdd1fc29aa868eabc132fd5320a77e43"} {"question" "q-104209" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038052/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d96e9c0c18cfc1671ca2507634917d13a07ac0f22b2225cb7e810ba8b2f8885c"} {"question" "q-104209" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038052/state.json" "file_sha256" "642ab815f48a1942583b110a577e3c48cdd1fc29aa868eabc132fd5320a77e43"} {"question" "q-104209" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\theta =\\frac{11\\pi }{8}"] ["math" "\\theta =\\frac{11\\pi }{8}r"] ["math" "r=\\frac{11\\pi }{8}"] ["math" "z=\\frac{11\\pi }{8}"] ["math" "z=\\frac{11\\pi }{8}\\theta "]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038052/state.json" "file_sha256" "642ab815f48a1942583b110a577e3c48cdd1fc29aa868eabc132fd5320a77e43"} {"question" "q-104209" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\theta =\\frac{11\\pi }{8}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038052/state.json" "file_sha256" "642ab815f48a1942583b110a577e3c48cdd1fc29aa868eabc132fd5320a77e43"} {"question" "q-104214" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038052/assets/c4d6b6fe6b594192040f6dc6c3a37b7eacff4387dc31d2d9e5f353fefd5c4663.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the surface shown above in cylindrical polar coordinates?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038052/state.json" "file_sha256" "642ab815f48a1942583b110a577e3c48cdd1fc29aa868eabc132fd5320a77e43"} {"question" "q-104214" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The position of a point in cylindrical polar coordinates is written as $(r,\\theta ,z),$ where \n\n- $r\\ge 0$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ are the usual plane polar coordinates, and\n\n- $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ is the usual Cartesian $z$-coordinate.\n\nThe picture shows the $xy$-plane.\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- Since the $z$-coordinate of any point on our surface equals $0,$ we must have $z=0$ for every point on the surface.\n\n- On the other hand, $r$ and $\\theta $ can vary arbitrarily in their respective domains.\n\nTherefore, the equation of the surface in cylindrical polar coordinates is $z=0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038052/state.json" "file_sha256" "642ab815f48a1942583b110a577e3c48cdd1fc29aa868eabc132fd5320a77e43"} {"question" "q-104214" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038052/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d96e9c0c18cfc1671ca2507634917d13a07ac0f22b2225cb7e810ba8b2f8885c"} {"question" "q-104214" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038052/state.json" "file_sha256" "642ab815f48a1942583b110a577e3c48cdd1fc29aa868eabc132fd5320a77e43"} {"question" "q-104214" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\theta =0"] ["math" "\\theta =z"] ["math" "r=0"] ["math" "z=0"] ["math" "z=r"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038052/state.json" "file_sha256" "642ab815f48a1942583b110a577e3c48cdd1fc29aa868eabc132fd5320a77e43"} {"question" "q-104214" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "z=0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038052/state.json" "file_sha256" "642ab815f48a1942583b110a577e3c48cdd1fc29aa868eabc132fd5320a77e43"} {"question" "q-104213" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038052/assets/fe283d8f0d0b78729a7fc7bda93e900e1f310de2dbbc62f23d8f8a7ff65af462.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the surface shown above in cylindrical polar coordinates?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038052/state.json" "file_sha256" "642ab815f48a1942583b110a577e3c48cdd1fc29aa868eabc132fd5320a77e43"} {"question" "q-104213" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The position of a point in cylindrical polar coordinates is written as $(r,\\theta ,z),$ where \n\n- $r\\ge 0$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ are the usual plane polar coordinates, and\n\n- $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ is the usual Cartesian $z$-coordinate.\n\nThe picture shows a plane parallel to the $xy$-plane.\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- Since the $z$-coordinate of any point on our surface equals $-1,$ we must have $z=-1$ for every point on the surface.\n\n- On the other hand, $r$ and $\\theta $ can vary arbitrarily in their respective domains.\n\nTherefore, the equation of the surface in cylindrical polar coordinates is $z=-1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038052/state.json" "file_sha256" "642ab815f48a1942583b110