t in cylindrical polar coordinates is written as $(r,\\theta ,z),$ where \n\n- $r\\ge 0$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ are the usual plane polar coordinates, and\n\n- $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ is the usual Cartesian $z$-coordinate.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our equation:\n\n$$\nz=3\n$$\n\nThis equation requires that $z$-coordinate of any point on our surface equals $3,$ while $r$ and $\\theta $ can vary arbitrarily in their respective domains. \n\nTherefore, our surface is the plane that is parallel to the $xy$-plane shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038052/assets/ab98ba68465b36fcc3c7ac2de4f50a8f3f16f5e7e10f2c08625cbd933c59f3c4.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038052/state.json" "file_sha256" "642ab815f48a1942583b110a577e3c48cdd1fc29aa868eabc132fd5320a77e43"} {"question" "q-104223" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038052/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d96e9c0c18cfc1671ca2507634917d13a07ac0f22b2225cb7e810ba8b2f8885c"} {"question" "q-104223" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038052/state.json" "file_sha256" "642ab815f48a1942583b110a577e3c48cdd1fc29aa868eabc132fd5320a77e43"} {"question" "q-104223" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "A circular cylinder"] ["text" "A cone"] ["text" "A half-cone"] ["text" "A half-plane that passes through the $z$-axis"] ["text" "A plane that is parallel to the $xy$-plane"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038052/state.json" "file_sha256" "642ab815f48a1942583b110a577e3c48cdd1fc29aa868eabc132fd5320a77e43"} {"question" "q-104223" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "A plane that is parallel to the $xy$-plane"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038052/state.json" "file_sha256" "642ab815f48a1942583b110a577e3c48cdd1fc29aa868eabc132fd5320a77e43"} {"question" "q-104229" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What surface is defined by the equation $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ in cylindrical polar coordinates?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038052/state.json" "file_sha256" "642ab815f48a1942583b110a577e3c48cdd1fc29aa868eabc132fd5320a77e43"} {"question" "q-104229" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The position of a point in cylindrical polar coordinates is written as $(r,\\theta ,z),$ where \n\n- $r\\ge 0$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ are the usual plane polar coordinates, and\n\n- $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ is the usual Cartesian $z$-coordinate.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our equation:\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}\n$$\n\nThis equation requires that if we project the vector $\\overset{\\to }{OP}$ onto the $xy$-plane for every point $P$ on our surface, the resulting vector makes an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ radians with the positive $x$-axis. So, we must have $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ for every point on the surface. On the other hand, $r$ and $z$ can vary arbitrarily in their respective domains.\n\nTherefore, our surface is a half-plane that passes through the $z$-axis, shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038052/assets/4eefd7f719ccb0c34407d6c02ef1adeb283f45e80ed6a95525661b84e39b3a8d.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038052/state.json" "file_sha256" "642ab815f48a1942583b110a577e3c48cdd1fc29aa868eabc132fd5320a77e43"} {"question" "q-104229" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038052/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d96e9c0c18cfc1671ca2507634917d13a07ac0f22b2225cb7e810ba8b2f8885c"} {"question" "q-104229" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038052/state.json" "file_sha256" "642ab815f48a1942583b110a577e3c48cdd1fc29aa868eabc132fd5320a77e43"} {"question" "q-104229" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "A circular sector"] ["text" "A cone"] ["text" "A half-plane that passes through the $z$-axis"] ["text" "A plane that is parallel to the $xy$-plane"] ["text" "A sphere"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038052/state.json" "file_sha256" "642ab815f48a1942583b110a577e3c48cdd1fc29aa868eabc132fd5320a77e43"} {"question" "q-104229" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "A half-plane that passes through the $z$-axis"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038052/state.json" "file_sha256" "642ab815f48a1942583b110a577e3c48cdd1fc29aa868eabc132fd5320a77e43"} {"question" "q-104219" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What surface is defined by the equation $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{8}$ in cylindrical polar coordinates?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038052/state.json" "file_sha256" "642ab815f48a1942583b110a577e3c48cdd1fc29aa868eabc132fd5320a77e43"} {"question" "q-104219" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The position of a point in cylindrical polar coordinates is written as $(r,\\theta ,z),$ where \n\n- $r\\ge 0$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ are the usual plane polar coordinates, and\n\n- $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ is the usual Cartesian $z$-coordinate.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our equation:\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{8}\n$$\n\nThis equation requires that if we project the vector $\\overset{\\to }{OP}$ onto the $xy$-plane for every point $P$ on our surface, the resulting vector makes an angle of $\\frac{3\\pi }{8}$ radians with the positive $x$-axis. So, we must have $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{8}$ for every point on the surface. On the other hand, $r$ and $z$ can vary arbitrarily in their respective domains.\n\nTherefore, our surface is a half-plane that passes through the $z$-axis, shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038052/assets/0118d67477cd0665d4ae5cbebbf3c3d6f3a6a24f4944977d4301cbaf99d3ecaa.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038052/state.json" "file_sha256" "642ab815f48a1942583b110a577e3c48cdd1fc29aa868eabc132fd5320a77e43"} {"question" "q-104219" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038052/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d96e9c0c18cfc1671ca2507634917d13a07ac0f22b2225cb7e810ba8b2f8885c"} {"question" "q-104219" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038052/state.json" "file_sha256" "642ab815f48a1942583b110a577e3c48cdd1fc29aa868eabc132fd5320a77e43"} {"question" "q-104219" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "A circular cylinder"] ["text" "A cone"] ["text" "A half-cone"] ["text" "A half-plane that passes through the $z$-axis"] ["text" "A plane that is parallel to the $xy$-plane"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038052/state.json" "file_sha256" "642ab815f48a1942583b110a577e3c48cdd1fc29aa868eabc132fd5320a77e43"} {"question" "q-104219" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "A half-plane that passes through the $z$-axis"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038052/state.json" "file_sha256" "642ab815f48a1942583b110a577e3c48cdd1fc29aa868eabc132fd5320a77e43"} {"question" "q-104225" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What surface is defined by the equation $r=4$ in cylindrical polar coordinates?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038052/state.json" "file_sha256" "642ab815f48a1942583b110a577e3c48cdd1fc29aa868eabc132fd5320a77e43"} {"question" "q-104225" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The position of a point in cylindrical polar coordinates is written as $(r,\\theta ,z),$ where \n\n- $r\\ge 0$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ are the usual plane polar coordinates, and\n\n- $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ is the usual Cartesian $z$-coordinate.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our equation:\n\n$$\nr=4\n$$\n\nThis equation requires that the distance from any point on the surface to the $z$-axis equals $4,$ while $\\theta $ and $z$ can vary arbitrarily in their respective domains. \n\nTherefore, our surface is the circular cylinder shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038052/assets/fc5c614a18a4cbaa50475c6612cc6b2c6458f9d5bed764b79ba52e624f1083c8.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038052/state.json" "file_sha256" "642ab815f48a1942583b110a577e3c48cdd1fc29aa868eabc132fd5320a77e43"} {"question" "q-104225" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038052/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d96e9c0c18cfc1671ca2507634917d13a07ac0f22b2225cb7e810ba8b2f8885c"} {"question" "q-104225" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038052/state.json" "file_sha256" "642ab815f48a1942583b110a577e3c48cdd1fc29aa868eabc132fd5320a77e43"} {"question" "q-104225" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "A circular cylinder"] ["text" "A cone"] ["text" "A half-cone"] ["text" "A half-plane that passes through the $z$-axis"] ["text" "A plane that is parallel to the $xy$-plane"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038052/state.json" "file_sha256" "642ab815f48a1942583b110a577e3c48cdd1fc29aa868eabc132fd5320a77e43"} {"question" "q-104225" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "A circular cylinder"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038052/state.json" "file_sha256" "642ab815f48a1942583b110a577e3c48cdd1fc29aa868eabc132fd5320a77e43"} {"question" "q-104227" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What surface is defined by the equation $z=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ in cylindrical polar coordinates?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038052/state.json" "file_sha256" "642ab815f48a1942583b110a577e3c48cdd1fc29aa868eabc132fd5320a77e43"} {"question" "q-104227" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The position of a point in cylindrical polar coordinates is written as $(r,\\theta ,z),$ where \n\n- $r\\ge 0$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ are the usual plane polar coordinates, and\n\n- $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ is the usual Cartesian $z$-coordinate.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our equation:\n\n$$\nz=\\frac{\\pi }{4}\n$$\n\nThis equation requires that $z$-coordinate of any point on our surface equals $\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ while $r$ and $\\theta $ can vary arbitrarily in their respective domains. \n\nTherefore, our surface is the plane that is parallel to the $xy$-plane shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038052/assets/99e84f48fa5fa03782c4e18d94c22c31bd39b902e2a289f5958afb7a963f7945.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038052/state.json" "file_sha256" "642ab815f48a1942583b110a577e3c48cdd1fc29aa868eabc132fd5320a77e43"} {"question" "q-104227" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038052/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d96e9c0c18cfc1671ca2507634917d13a07ac0f22b2225cb7e810ba8b2f8885c"} {"question" "q-104227" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038052/state.json" "file_sha256" "642ab815f48a1942583b110a577e3c48cdd1fc29aa868eabc132fd5320a77e43"} {"question" "q-104227" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "A circular cylinder"] ["text" "A half-cone"] ["text" "A half-plane that passes through the $z$-axis"] ["text" "A plane that is parallel to the $xy$-plane"] ["text" "A sphere"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038052/state.json" "file_sha256" "642ab815f48a1942583b110a577e3c48cdd1fc29aa868eabc132fd5320a77e43"} {"question" "q-104227" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "A plane that is parallel to the $xy$-plane"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038052/state.json" "file_sha256" "642ab815f48a1942583b110a577e3c48cdd1fc29aa868eabc132fd5320a77e43"} {"question" "q-104329" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038052/assets/a89469b49ecb4f40ba8378e1ce5404f5a1cf0eb4e78922529d9dce368b28c82d.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the surface shown above in cylindrical polar coordinates?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038052/state.json" "file_sha256" "642ab815f48a1942583b110a577e3c48cdd1fc29aa868eabc132fd5320a77e43"} {"question" "q-104329" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The position of a point in cylindrical polar coordinates is written as $(r,\\theta ,z),$ where \n\n- $r\\ge 0$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ are the usual plane polar coordinates, and\n\n- $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ is the usual Cartesian $z$-coordinate.\n\nThe picture shows a circular cone whose axis of symmetry is the $z$-axis. \n\nWe note the following:\n\n- From the diagram, the angle between the position vector of any point on the surface and the $z$-axis must be equal $\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ \n\nThe tangent of this angle equals the ratio between the distance $r$ from the point to the $z$-axis and the distance $z$ from the point to the $xy$-plane. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{r}{z} & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\ \\frac{r}{z} & =1 \\\\ z & =r.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- On the other hand, $\\theta $ can vary arbitrarily in its domain.\n\nFinally, recall that $r\\ge 0.$ Since we wish to include both half-cones, we need to include $z=-r$ as well. We can combine the two equations by squaring both sides.\n\nTherefore, the equation of the surface in cylindrical polar coordinates is ${z}^{2}={r}^{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038052/state.json" "file_sha256" "642ab815f48a1942583b110a577e3c48cdd1fc29aa868eabc132fd5320a77e43"} {"question" "q-104329" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038052/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d96e9c0c18cfc1671ca2507634917d13a07ac0f22b2225cb7e810ba8b2f8885c"} {"question" "q-104329" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038052/state.json" "file_sha256" "642ab815f48a1942583b110a577e3c48cdd1fc29aa868eabc132fd5320a77e43"} {"question" "q-104329" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "z=2r"] ["math" "z=\\sqrt{2}r"] ["math" "{z}^{2}=2{r}^{2}"] ["math" "{z}^{2}=\\sqrt{2}{r}^{2}"] ["math" "{z}^{2}={r}^{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038052/state.json" "file_sha256" "642ab815f48a1942583b110a577e3c48cdd1fc29aa868eabc132fd5320a77e43"} {"question" "q-104329" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{z}^{2}={r}^{2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038052/state.json" "file_sha256" "642ab815f48a1942583b110a577e3c48cdd1fc29aa868eabc132fd5320a77e43"} {"question" "q-104243" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038052/assets/446989d58605132a7e8e361b12127341c490845d893a792662b41c3d086e71e6.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the surface shown above in cylindrical polar coordinates?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038052/state.json" "file_sha256" "642ab815f48a1942583b110a577e3c48cdd1fc29aa868eabc132fd5320a77e43"} {"question" "q-104243" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The position of a point in cylindrical polar coordinates is written as $(r,\\theta ,z),$ where \n\n- $r\\ge 0$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ are the usual plane polar coordinates, and\n\n- $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ is the usual Cartesian $z$-coordinate.\n\nThe picture shows a circular half-cone whose axis of symmetry is the $z$-axis. \n\nWe note the following:\n\n- From the diagram, the angle between the position vector of any point on the surface and the $z$-axis must be equal $\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ \n\nThe tangent of this angle equals the ratio between the distance $r$ from the point to the $z$-axis and the distance $z$ from the point to the $xy$-plane. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{r}{z} & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\ \\frac{r}{z} & =1 \\\\ z & =r.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- On the other hand, $\\theta $ can vary arbitrarily in its domain.\n\nTherefore, the equation of the surface in cylindrical polar coordinates is $z=r.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038052/state.json" "file_sha256" "642ab815f48a1942583b110a577e3c48cdd1fc29aa868eabc132fd5320a77e43"} {"question" "q-104243" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038052/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d96e9c0c18cfc1671ca2507634917d13a07ac0f22b2225cb7e810ba8b2f8885c"} {"question" "q-104243" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038052/state.json" "file_sha256" "642ab815f48a1942583b110a577e3c48cdd1fc29aa868eabc132fd5320a77e43"} {"question" "q-104243" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "z=\\frac{r}{\\pi }"] ["math" "z=\\pi r"] ["math" "z=r"] ["math" "{z}^{2}=4{r}^{2}"] ["math" "{z}^{2}=\\frac{{r}^{2}}{\\pi }"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038052/state.json" "file_sha256" "642ab815f48a1942583b110a577e3c48cdd1fc29aa868eabc132fd5320a77e43"} {"question" "q-104243" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "z=r"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038052/state.json" "file_sha256" "642ab815f48a1942583b110a577e3c48cdd1fc29aa868eabc132fd5320a77e43"} {"question" "q-104236" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What surface is defined by the equation ${z}^{2}=4{r}^{2}$ in cylindrical polar coordinates?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038052/state.json" "file_sha256" "642ab815f48a1942583b110a577e3c48cdd1fc29aa868eabc132fd5320a77e43"} {"question" "q-104236" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The position of a point in cylindrical polar coordinates is written as $(r,\\theta ,z),$ where \n\n- $r\\ge 0$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ are the usual plane polar coordinates, and\n\n- $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ is the usual Cartesian $z$-coordinate.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our equation:\n\n$$\n{z}^{2}=4{r}^{2}\n$$\n\nThis equation requires that the square of the distance from any point on the surface to the $xy$-plane equals $4$ times the square of the distance from that point to the $z$-axis. In other words, the tangent of the angle between any position vector on the surface and the $z$-axis must be equal to\n\n$$\n\\frac{r}{z}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{r}^{2}}{{z}^{2}}}=\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{{r}^{2}}{4{r}^{2}}}=\\pm \\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nSince the tangent of this angle can be a positive or negative number, the $z$-coordinate can also be positive or negative.\n\nOn the other hand, $\\theta $ can vary arbitrarily in its domain. \n\nTherefore, our surface is the cone shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038052/assets/7c8d0cefa5eb8da76f6cba59570670e4078c278c801aeb12344dce3183733149.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038052/state.json" "file_sha256" "642ab815f48a1942583b110a577e3c48cdd1fc29aa868eabc132fd5320a77e43"} {"question" "q-104236" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038052/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d96e9c0c18cfc1671ca2507634917d13a07ac0f22b2225cb7e810ba8b2f8885c"} {"question" "q-104236" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038052/state.json" "file_sha256" "642ab815f48a1942583b110a577e3c48cdd1fc29aa868eabc132fd5320a77e43"} {"question" "q-104236" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "A circular cylinder"] ["text" "A cone"] ["text" "A half-plane that passes through the $z$-axis"] ["text" "A plane that is parallel to the $xy$-plane"] ["text" "A sphere"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038052/state.json" "file_sha256" "642ab815f48a1942583b110a577e3c48cdd1fc29aa868eabc132fd5320a77e43"} {"question" "q-104236" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "A cone"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038052/state.json" "file_sha256" "642ab815f48a1942583b110a577e3c48cdd1fc29aa868eabc132fd5320a77e43"} {"question" "q-104335" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What surface is defined by the equation $z=3r$ in cylindrical polar coordinates?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038052/state.json" "file_sha256" "642ab815f48a1942583b110a577e3c48cdd1fc29aa868eabc132fd5320a77e43"} {"question" "q-104335" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The position of a point in cylindrical polar coordinates is written as $(r,\\theta ,z),$ where \n\n- $r\\ge 0$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ are the usual plane polar coordinates, and\n\n- $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ is the usual Cartesian $z$-coordinate.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our equation:\n\n$$\nz=3r\n$$\n\nThis equation requires that the distance from any point on the surface to the $xy$-plane equals $3$ times the distance from that point to the $z$-axis. In other words, the tangent of the angle between any position vector on the surface and the $z$-axis must be equal to\n\n$$\n\\frac{r}{z}=\\frac{r}{3r}=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, $\\theta $ can vary arbitrarily in its domain. \n\nTherefore, our surface is the half-cone shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038052/assets/69978d3f1026cf83019924c44d66f0e510ce8c84b35d10c77c270b87809b30aa.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038052/state.json" "file_sha256" "642ab815f48a1942583b110a577e3c48cdd1fc29aa868eabc132fd5320a77e43"} {"question" "q-104335" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038052/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d96e9c0c18cfc1671ca2507634917d13a07ac0f22b2225cb7e810ba8b2f8885c"} {"question" "q-104335" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038052/state.json" "file_sha256" "642ab815f48a1942583b110a577e3c48cdd1fc29aa868eabc132fd5320a77e43"} {"question" "q-104335" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "A circular cylinder"] ["text" "A half-cone"] ["text" "A half-plane that passes through the $z$-axis"] ["text" "A plane that is parallel to the $xy$-plane"] ["text" "A sphere"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038052/state.json" "file_sha256" "642ab815f48a1942583b110a577e3c48cdd1fc29aa868eabc132fd5320a77e43"} {"question" "q-104335" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "A half-cone"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038052/state.json" "file_sha256" "642ab815f48a1942583b110a577e3c48cdd1fc29aa868eabc132fd5320a77e43"} {"question" "q-104241" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What surface is defined by the equation $z=\\frac{1}{5}r$ in cylindrical polar coordinates?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038052/state.json" "file_sha256" "642ab815f48a1942583b110a577e3c48cdd1fc29aa868eabc132fd5320a77e43"} {"question" "q-104241" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The position of a point in cylindrical polar coordinates is written as $(r,\\theta ,z),$ where \n\n- $r\\ge 0$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ are the usual plane polar coordinates, and\n\n- $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ is the usual Cartesian $z$-coordinate.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our equation:\n\n$$\nz=\\frac{1}{5}r\n$$\n\nThis equation requires that the distance from any point on the surface to the $xy$-plane equals one-fifth the distance from that point to the $z$-axis. In other words, the tangent of the angle between any position vector on the surface and the $z$-axis must be equal to\n\n$$\n\\frac{r}{z}=\\frac{r}{(\\frac{1}{5}r)}=5.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, $\\theta $ can vary arbitrarily in its domain. \n\nTherefore, our surface is the half-cone shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038052/assets/69978d3f1026cf83019924c44d66f0e510ce8c84b35d10c77c270b87809b30aa.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038052/state.json" "file_sha256" "642ab815f48a1942583b110a577e3c48cdd1fc29aa868eabc132fd5320a77e43"} {"question" "q-104241" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038052/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d96e9c0c18cfc1671ca2507634917d13a07ac0f22b2225cb7e810ba8b2f8885c"} {"question" "q-104241" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038052/state.json" "file_sha256" "642ab815f48a1942583b110a577e3c48cdd1fc29aa868eabc132fd5320a77e43"} {"question" "q-104241" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "A circular cylinder"] ["text" "A half-cone"] ["text" "A half-plane that passes through the $z$-axis"] ["text" "A plane that is parallel to the $xy$-plane"] ["text" "A sphere"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038052/state.json" "file_sha256" "642ab815f48a1942583b110a577e3c48cdd1fc29aa868eabc132fd5320a77e43"} {"question" "q-104241" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "A half-cone"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038052/state.json" "file_sha256" "642ab815f48a1942583b110a577e3c48cdd1fc29aa868eabc132fd5320a77e43"} {"question" "e-8809" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038052/assets/1ea30ad7159ada825af44e58f3747f378815e572c0b18d3e8916fd2a6eec58cf.png)\n\n \n\nWhat is the equation of the surface shown above in cylindrical polar coordinates?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038052/example-8809.json" "file_sha256" "73b7f8b974c1fa79afbfdc92b8c7fa6f1b86a6285bbed4b8e29239ab4bbbe095"} {"question" "e-8809" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The position of a point in cylindrical polar coordinates is written as $(r,\\theta,z),$ where \n\n- $r\\geq 0$ and $\\theta\\in [0,2\\pi)$ are the usual plane polar coordinates, and\n\n- $z\\in (-\\infty, \\infty)$ is the usual Cartesian $z$-coordinate.\n\nThe picture shows a circular cylinder of radius $5$ whose axis of symmetry is the $z$-axis. \n\nWe note the following:\n\n- Since the distance from any point on our surface to the $z$-axis equals $5,$ we must have $r=5$ for every point on the surface.\n\n- On the other hand, $\\theta$ and $z$ can vary arbitrarily in their respective domains.\n\nTherefore, the equation of the surface in cylindrical polar coordinates is $r = 5.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038052/example-8809.json" "file_sha256" "73b7f8b974c1fa79afbfdc92b8c7fa6f1b86a6285bbed4b8e29239ab4bbbe095"} {"question" "e-8808" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What surface is defined by the equation $\\theta=3$ in cylindrical polar coordinates?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038052/example-8808.json" "file_sha256" "12d2b20b4d6f14cf245af3b9a53b088eb021f757d01704906f11fb1ad488439c"} {"question" "e-8808" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The position of a point in cylindrical polar coordinates is written as $(r,\\theta,z),$ where \n\n- $r\\geq 0$ and $\\theta\\in [0,2\\pi)$ are the usual plane polar coordinates, and\n\n- $z\\in (-\\infty, \\infty)$ is the usual Cartesian $z$-coordinate.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our equation:\n\n$$\n\\theta=3\n$$\n\nThis equation requires that if we project the vector $\\overrightarrow{OP}$ onto the $xy$-plane for every point $P$ on our surface, the resulting vector makes an angle of $3$ radians with the positive $x$-axis. So, we must have $\\theta=3$ for every point on the surface. On the other hand, $r$ and $z$ can vary arbitrarily in their respective domains. \n\nTherefore, our surface is a half-plane that passes through the $z$-axis, shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038052/assets/2908fb8953f692c596822ecf3fb6454cc5f02f1620f3936d2f5a198d0e9a380d.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038052/example-8808.json" "file_sha256" "12d2b20b4d6f14cf245af3b9a53b088eb021f757d01704906f11fb1ad488439c"} {"question" "e-8893" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What surface is defined by the equation $z=4r$ in cylindrical polar coordinates?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038052/example-8893.json" "file_sha256" "2d53314aa0ed7529c6fa1090b292245b9a4ad170ddd3b2be5839c8396196ff72"} {"question" "e-8893" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The position of a p